From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JalenLin <jalen.lin@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:04:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/22] arm64: smp_spin_table: allow an MMIO cpu-release-addr

The spin-table boot protocol says cpu-release-addr is a memory location, and
mainline maps it with ioremap_cache and stores 64 bits to it. Some SoCs point
it at a register instead: the Realtek RTD129x has its secondary CPUs wait on
a 32-bit register in the SB2 block at 0x9801aa44, and mapping that cacheable
and storing a doubleword across it does not bring them up.

Pick the access by asking memblock whether the address is mapped RAM. When it
is not, map it as device memory and store one 32-bit word -- which is what
Realtek's own BSP does in drivers/soc/realtek/rtd129x/rtd129x_spin_table.c.
Platforms whose release address really is RAM are unaffected.

Source: https://github.com/JalenLin/BPI-W2-linux

Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/smp_spin_table.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/smp_spin_table.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/smp_spin_table.c
index 49029eace3ad..0566b68815c2 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/smp_spin_table.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/smp_spin_table.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
+#include <linux/memblock.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/smp.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
@@ -71,6 +72,29 @@ static int smp_spin_table_cpu_prepare(unsigned int cpu)
 	if (!cpu_release_addr[cpu])
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * Some SoCs point cpu-release-addr at an MMIO register rather than at
+	 * RAM -- the Realtek RTD129x writes the secondary entry point to a
+	 * 32-bit register in its SB2 block. Mapping that cacheable and storing
+	 * 64 bits to it does not work, so map it as device memory and store a
+	 * single 32-bit word. The holding pen is well inside the low 4 GiB on
+	 * such systems.
+	 */
+	if (!memblock_is_map_memory(cpu_release_addr[cpu])) {
+		void __iomem *reg;
+
+		reg = ioremap(cpu_release_addr[cpu], sizeof(u32));
+		if (!reg)
+			return -ENOMEM;
+
+		writel_relaxed(pa_holding_pen, reg);
+		iounmap(reg);
+
+		sev();
+
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * The cpu-release-addr may or may not be inside the linear mapping.
 	 * As ioremap_cache will either give us a new mapping or reuse the
